\title[EML differential equations and Airy's equation]{Exponential--Logarithmic Differential Equations:\\
Closed-Form Solvability and the Failure of Kovacic's\\ First Two Cases for Airy's Equation}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 4, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a self-contained theory of \emph{exponential--logarithmic} (EML) functions --- the real
functions generated from the identity, real constants, addition, multiplication, inversion, $\exp$
and $\log$ --- and use it to analyse the closed-form solvability of the second-order linear equation
$y'' = r\,y$ with polynomial coefficient $r$, with the Airy equation $y'' = x\,y$ as the guiding case.

Three groups of results are established. First, a syntax for EML functions together with a symbolic
derivative operator and a pointwise regularity predicate, for which we prove correctness of symbolic
differentiation: on its regularity locus, an EML expression defines a differentiable function whose
analytic derivative is exactly the function defined by the symbolically differentiated expression,
and regularity propagates to the derivative. As an application we give the complete solution theory
of the first-order linear equation $y'=c\,y$. Second, we establish the algebraic obstructions
underlying Kovacic's first case: for polynomial $r$ of odd degree the Riccati equation $u'+u^2=r$ has
no rational solution; a polynomial Riccati solution has $\deg r = 2\deg u$ and $\lc r = (\lc u)^2$;
and for $r \neq 0$ the equation $y''=ry$ has no nonzero rational solution. Consequently Airy's
equation admits no EML solution of exponential type $y=e^{F}$ with $F'$ rational. The odd-degree
hypothesis is sharp: for $r=x^2+1$ one has the solution $y=e^{x^2/2}$. Third, we treat Kovacic's
second case: the second symmetric power equation $v'''=4rv'+2r'v$ for products of solutions, the
exact leading coefficient $\bigl(4(\deg P-\deg Q)+2\bigr)\lc(P)\lc(Q)^3$ of the cleared right-hand
side for $r=x$, and the conclusion that $v'''=4xv'+2v$ has no nonzero polynomial and no nonzero
rational solution, so that no product of two solutions of Airy's equation is a nonzero rational
function. The transcendence statement thereby reduces to two arithmetic facts: $2p$ is even while
$2q+1$ is odd, and $4k+2 \neq 0$ for every integer $k$.
\end{abstract}

